# What must not enter the build context.
#
# The first three groups are correctness, not size: `.venv/` holds a Python
# built for the host and would shadow the one installed in the image, `.env`
# holds credentials that would be baked into a layer readable by anyone who
# pulls the image, and `.git/` carries the whole history of a repository whose
# specs are larger than its code.

.venv/
venv/
.env
.env.*
.git/
.github/

# Build and cache output. Reproducible from source, and stale in an image.
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.hypothesis/
.import_linter_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/

# Design history. It governs the code and does not run.
specs/
docs/
.specify/
.claude/
*.md

# …except this one, which is a **build input**. `pyproject.toml` declares
# `readme = "README.md"`, so hatchling reads it while building the wheel, and
# without it `uv pip install .` fails with a file-not-found that names a path
# the developer can see on disk — the most confusing shape a build error has.
#
# The exclusion above is still right: every other Markdown file in this
# repository is design history and none of it runs. This is the one that is not
# documentation at all from the build's point of view; it is metadata the
# packaging backend requires.
!README.md

# Test fixtures include whole PDFs. The image runs a service; it does not run
# the suite, and a scanned invoice is not a runtime dependency.
tests/
datasets/

# Local editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
